        "value": "Binary politeness distinction",
        "source": "wals-v2020.5",
        "note": "<div class=\"Example\"><table class=\"example\"><tr><td><p><i>(o)mudou/ (o)dou</i></p></td><td><p>2nd sg. hon.</p></td><td><p>- used for address of brother or sister of opposite sex,</p><p>elder siblings of same sex; etymologically, this is a 2nd</p><p>paucal pronoun</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><i>(o)mudrau/ (o)drau</i></p></td><td><p>2nd sg. hon.</p></td><td><p>- etymology is actually 2nd du, used for address of</p><p>mother-in-law, father-in-law</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><i>(o)munuu/ (o)nuu</i></p></td><td><p>2nd sg. hon.</p></td><td><p>- used for the address of the village chief or generally</p><p>old persons who deserve respect, especially the short</p><p>form <i>(o)nuu</i> is reserved for the village chief</p></td></tr></table></div>",
        "scale": "wals-v2020.5"
      }
